layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | The
    strong runner
    | is a small background program that lets Buildyboy start Claude Code on a machine. It is optional: you only need it
    | to launch
    a(href="/docs/jobs") jobs
    | or use
    a(href="/docs/composer#spec-ify") Spec-ify
    | . Watching and answering the sessions you start yourself works without it.

  h2#how How it works, and why it is safe
  p
    | The runner opens a connection
    strong from
    | your machine
    strong to
    | the server and waits. Nothing on your machine listens for incoming connections. When you launch a job, the server
    | sends it down that connection; the runner checks it and starts Claude Code.
  p The runner protects your machine with three rules the server cannot change:
  ul
    li
      strong Allowed roots.
      | It only runs jobs inside folders you list in its settings file on that machine. A job for any other folder is
      | refused.
    li
      strong Only Claude.
      | It only ever starts
      code claude
      | with a checked list of options, without a shell, so a prompt cannot sneak in another command.
    li
      strong Verify commands are the prompt's own.
      | When you
      a(href="/docs/jobs#verify") verify
      | a job, it only runs commands that were written in that job's Done when, after you confirmed them.

  h2#requirements What you need
  ul
    li The machine is paired (see Pairing machines).
    li Node.js 24 and a copy of the Buildyboy code on the machine, with its dependencies installed (the three commands below).
    li
      code claude
      | installed and logged in on that machine, so it runs without asking anything.
  pre
    code
      | git clone https://github.com/Narcis13/buildyboy
      | cd buildyboy
      | npm ci

  h2#config Step 1 · Write runner.json
  p
    | The runner reads
    code ~/.buildyboy/runner.json
    | (on Windows,
    code %USERPROFILE%\.buildyboy\runner.json
    | ). The installer does not create it, so write it yourself. The smallest useful file lists the folders jobs may run
    | in:
  pre
    code
      | {
      |   "allowedRoots": ["C:\\work", "C:\\Users\\me\\code"],
      |   "concurrency": 2
      | }
  p On Linux, WSL or macOS:
  pre
    code
      | {
      |   "allowedRoots": ["/home/me/code"],
      |   "concurrency": 2
      | }
  p All the settings:
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Key
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Default
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Meaning
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code allowedRoots
          td(data-part="td") required
          td(data-part="td") Folders jobs may run in, and everything under them. At least one. Windows paths need doubled backslashes in JSON.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code concurrency
          td(data-part="td") 2
          td(data-part="td") How many jobs may run at the same time on this machine.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code claudePath
          td(data-part="td") claude on the PATH
          td(data-part="td") The full path of the claude program, if it is not found automatically (on Windows, point it at claude.exe).
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code pluginDirs
          td(data-part="td") none
          td(data-part="td") Extra Claude Code plugin folders to load in every job.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code idleExitSeconds
          td(data-part="td") 120
          td(data-part="td") How long a finished job waits for a follow-up message before it ends.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td")
            code stopGraceSeconds
          td(data-part="td") 10
          td(data-part="td") After you press Stop, how long to wait before forcing the job to quit.
  p The file is read when the runner starts. After changing it, restart the runner.

  h2#start Step 2 · Start the runner
  h3#start-by-hand By hand (to try it)
  p From the folder where you cloned Buildyboy:
  pre
    code
      | node cli/bb.ts runner
  p It prints its allowed roots, its concurrency and the claude version it found, then "connected to …". Leave the window open; the runner stops when you close it. On the Machines page the machine now shows Runner: connected.
  h3#service-linux As a service on Linux or WSL
  p
    | Run the installer again with the runner switch, and tell it where your copy of the code is with BB_REPO. Note that
    | BB_REPO goes after the pipe, in front of bash, so the installer sees it. If the machine is already
    | paired, leave out the code; it is not paired again.
  pre
    code
      | curl -fsSL {{ site.url }}/install.sh | BB_REPO=$HOME/buildyboy bash -s -- --runner
  p
    | This creates and starts a user service named
    code buildyboy-runner
    | that restarts by itself. Check it with:
  pre
    code
      | systemctl --user status buildyboy-runner.service
      | journalctl --user -u buildyboy-runner.service -f
  p To keep it running after you log out (recommended on a server):
  pre
    code
      | loginctl enable-linger $USER
  h3#service-windows As a background task on Windows
  p In PowerShell:
  pre
    code
      | $env:BB_RUNNER='1'; $env:BB_REPO='C:\path\to\buildyboy'; irm {{ site.url }}/install.ps1 | iex
  p
    | This registers a scheduled task named
    code BuildyboyRunner
    | that starts at logon and restarts itself if it crashes, and starts it right away. If a scheduled task cannot be
    | created, it falls back to a shortcut in your Startup folder. The task keeps no log; to see what the runner is
    | doing, stop the task and run
    code node cli/bb.ts runner
    | in a terminal.
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        | Remember: Windows and WSL are separate machines. If you want jobs in both, set up a runner in both, each with its
        | own runner.json.

  h2#check Step 3 · Check it
  ol
    li
      | Open
      a(href="/docs/machines") Machines
      | : the machine says
      code Runner: connected
      | and its bay on the Floor says
      code runner online
      | .
    li
      | Launch a small job, for example the prompt "Say hello and list the files in this folder" with a $0.50 budget, in a
      | folder inside an allowed root.
    li Watch it go from queued to running to succeeded on its job page.

  h2#offline When the runner or the server goes away
  ul
    li
      strong The runner is stopped or the machine is off:
      | new jobs wait in the queue and start when it is back.
    li
      strong The connection drops during a job:
      | the runner keeps working and reconnects by itself, retrying every few seconds up to every 30 seconds. Output is
      | kept and sent when the connection is back. If it is gone for more than 2 minutes, the job is marked lost; it is
      | corrected if the runner later reports how it ended.
    li
      strong You stop the runner while jobs run:
      | its jobs are stopped ("runner shutting down").

  h2#problems Common problems
  dl
    dt "runner config not found"
    dd
      | There is no runner.json yet. Create it (Step 1). Until then a runner service keeps restarting.
    dt "cwd is outside the runner's allowed roots"
    dd The job's folder is not under any allowedRoots entry. Add the parent folder to runner.json and restart the runner, or launch in a folder that is.
    dt "'claude' not found on PATH"
    dd Set claudePath in runner.json to the full path of claude (claude.exe on Windows).
    dt "not paired"
    dd
      | The machine has no Buildyboy access key. Pair it first (see
      a(href="/docs/machines") Pairing machines
      | ).
    dt The job stays queued
    dd The runner is not connected (check Machines), all its slots are busy, or another job is already running in the same repository.
